  "step_2": "The individual considered the outcomes and probabilities of both lotteries. They noted that Lottery A offers a certain 5€ win, while Lottery B offers a 99.9% chance of 0€ and a 0.1% chance of 5000€. The individual described an 'instinctive preference' for Lottery B. Their justification centers on the idea that the 5€ guaranteed win is not significantly impactful (i.e., 'not so high that it makes a big difference'), while the small chance of winning 5000€ makes the risk of Lottery B seem attractive. The individual explicitly frames their choice as being driven by the appeal of a large potential gain despite the low probability, even though the guaranteed small gain is certain. The deliberation focuses on the attractiveness of the large outcome (5000€) in Lottery B and the relative insignificance of the small guaranteed gain (5€), rather than on expected value or risk aversion per se.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires comparing the maximum magnitude outcome weighted by its probability. In this case, both lotteries yield the same weighted maximum (5€), so the reason would be indifferent. However, the individual clearly preferred Lottery B, not due to indifference, but because of the appeal of the large potential gain (5000€) despite its low probability. This suggests that the individual’s justification is based on the magnitude of the maximum outcome (5000€) and its attractiveness, even though the weighted value is equal. While the individual did consider the maximum outcome (5000€) and its probability (0.1%), their reasoning is not based on computing or comparing the weighted maximum magnitude. Instead, they are motivated by the psychological appeal of a large win, which goes beyond the formal rule of the reason. The individual did not use the decision reason, because they did not compare the weighted maximum magnitudes (which are equal), nor did they base their choice on which lottery has the more favorable weighted maximum. Their choice was driven by an intuitive preference for the high-stakes possibility, not by a rule-based evaluation of weighted maximums. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

